        <p>Be honest with your scoring. An agency that scores well on portfolio but poorly on process will likely miss deadlines. An agency that scores well on price but poorly on technical capability will build you a site that is slow, hard to maintain, and expensive to fix later. The weighted total gives you a clearer picture than any single factor.</p>

        <h2>Red Flags That Should Make You Walk Away</h2>
        <p>Some warning signs are deal-breakers. If you see any of these, keep looking.</p>
        <p><strong>No discovery process.</strong> An agency that jumps straight to design without asking detailed questions about your business, audience, and goals is not doing strategy. They are doing decoration. Design without strategy is just art. You need design that converts.</p>
        <p><strong>Vague timelines and process.</strong> If the agency says "a few weeks" or "it depends" without defining milestones, review checkpoints, and deliverables, they do not have a process. A project without a process is a project without accountability. You will spend months chasing updates and wondering what is happening.</p>
        <p><strong>Rock-bottom pricing.</strong> A custom website for $500 or $1,000 is not realistic. Quality work requires research, custom design, clean development, testing, and revision rounds. At that price point, the agency is either using a pre-built template and calling it custom, outsourcing to cheap overseas labor, or planning to hit you with hidden fees later. <a href="/blog/why-cheap-websites-cost-money/">Cheap websites cost more in the long run</a> because they convert poorly, rank poorly, and need to be rebuilt within a year.</p>
        <p><strong>No discussion of mobile, speed, or SEO.</strong> Over 60 percent of web traffic is mobile. Google ranks sites based on page speed and Core Web Vitals. If the agency does not mention responsive design, performance optimization, or search engine optimization during the first conversation, they are building for 2015, not 2026.</p>
        <p><strong>Lock-in clauses and ownership issues.</strong> You should own your website, your code, and your design files after final payment. If the agency retains ownership, builds on a proprietary platform you cannot leave, or refuses to give you access to files and databases, you are renting your own website. That is not acceptable.</p>
        <p><strong>Poor communication during the sales process.</strong> How an agency communicates before you pay is the best preview of how they will communicate after. If they are slow to respond, vague in their answers, dismissive of your questions, or make you chase every update during the sales process, it will only get worse once they have your deposit.</p>
        <p><strong>Unrealistic timelines.</strong> A complete custom website in 30 days is not realistic for quality work. Discovery, design, development, content, testing, and revision rounds take time. A realistic timeline for a custom small business website is six to ten weeks. Complex projects take three to six months. Agencies that promise impossibly fast delivery are cutting corners.</p>
        <p><strong>No post-launch plan.</strong> Launch is not the finish line. It is the starting line. A website needs security updates, performance monitoring, content updates, and continuous optimization. If the agency treats launch as the end of the relationship and has no plan for ongoing support, you will be left with a site that slowly degrades.</p>

        <h2>Questions That Reveal Whether an Agency Is the Right Fit</h2>
        <p>The questions you ask during the discovery call separate professionals from pretenders. Here are the questions that reveal the most.</p>
        <p><strong>"Walk me through your process from discovery to launch."</strong> A good agency has a clear, step-by-step process with defined milestones. They can explain what happens at each stage, what they need from you, and what you will receive. A bad agency gives a vague answer like "we design, then we build, then we launch."</p>
        <p><strong>"Tell me about a project that went wrong. What happened and how did you handle it?"</strong> Every agency has had a project go sideways. The ones who claim otherwise are lying or inexperienced. What matters is how they handled it. Did they take responsibility? Did they find a solution? Did they learn from it? This question reveals character and problem-solving ability.</p>
        <p><strong>"How do you handle scope changes and tradeoffs?"</strong> Projects change. You will think of new features mid-build. A good agency has a clear change order process and can explain tradeoffs honestly. A bad agency says yes to everything and then delivers late, over budget, or with corners cut.</p>
        <p><strong>"What would make you turn down this project?"</strong> This is a powerful question. An agency that says yes to every project is desperate or lacks discernment. A good agency knows their strengths and will tell you if your timeline, budget, or requirements are not a fit. That honesty is a green flag.</p>
        <p><strong>"How do you measure success after launch?"</strong> If the agency cannot define success in measurable terms — leads generated, conversion rate improved, rankings gained — they are not accountable to results. You want an agency that cares about what happens after the site goes live, not just whether it looks good on launch day.</p>
        <p><strong>"Who will actually be working on my project?"</strong> Some agencies sell you on their senior team and then hand the work to junior contractors you never meet. Ask who does the design, who does the development, and who manages the project. Meet the actual team if possible.</p>

        <h2>How to Compare Proposals Without Getting Lost in the Details</h2>
        <p>Once you have talked to three to five agencies, you will have a stack of proposals that are hard to compare. One agency quotes $4,000. Another quotes $12,000. A third quotes $8,000 but with a different scope. Here is how to cut through the confusion.</p>
        <p>First, normalize the scope. Create a simple spreadsheet with the same categories for every proposal: number of pages, custom design vs template, content strategy, SEO setup, revision rounds, timeline, and post-launch support. This forces each proposal into the same structure so you can see what is actually included.</p>
        <p>Second, look for hidden costs. Does the price include copywriting, or is that extra? Does it include stock photography or custom photography? Does it include hosting setup and domain configuration? Does it include training on how to update the site? Some agencies quote a low base price and then add fees for everything else. Others quote a higher price that includes everything. The all-inclusive quote is often the better value.</p>
        <p>Third, evaluate the proposal on clarity, not just price. A proposal that is three pages long, clearly structured, and easy to understand reflects an agency that thinks clearly and communicates well. A proposal that is vague, full of jargon, and hard to follow reflects an agency that may be disorganized or trying to hide something.</p>
        <p>Finally, trust your instincts about fit. You will be working with this agency for weeks or months. You will have difficult conversations about tradeoffs, delays, and disagreements. Do you trust this team to handle those moments professionally? Do they make you feel more informed as the conversation continues, or more confused? The right agency should feel like a partner, not a vendor.</p>

