        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <h3>How much should I expect to pay for a custom website in 2026?</h3>
        <p>For a quality custom website built by a professional agency, expect to pay between $4,500 and $15,000 for most small to medium business projects. The exact price depends on the number of pages, custom functionality, content needs, and SEO requirements. <a href="/blog/how-much-does-a-website-cost/">Read our full pricing breakdown</a> for a detailed comparison of every option.</p>

        <h3>Should I hire a freelancer or an agency?</h3>
        <p>It depends on your project complexity and risk tolerance. Freelancers are less expensive and work well for simple sites with clear requirements. Agencies offer more accountability, broader expertise, and better support for complex projects. If your website is a core part of your growth strategy, an agency is usually the safer choice.</p>

        <h3>How long does a custom website take to build?</h3>
        <p>A typical custom small business website takes six to ten weeks from kickoff to launch. Complex projects with custom functionality, e-commerce, or integrations can take three to six months. Be wary of agencies that promise a custom site in under four weeks — they are likely cutting corners or using templates.</p>

        <h3>What is the difference between a custom website and a template?</h3>
        <p>A custom website is designed and built specifically for your brand, audience, and conversion goals. Every element is crafted to serve your business. A template is a pre-designed layout that is modified with your colors and logo. Templates are faster and cheaper but limit your ability to differentiate, optimize, and scale. <a href="/blog/custom-website-vs-template-which-is-better/">Read our full comparison</a> to decide which is right for you.</p>

        <h3>Do I need to write the content myself?</h3>
        <p>Some agencies include professional copywriting in their proposal. Others expect you to provide all content. This is a critical question to ask upfront, because content is what typically holds up most projects. If you are not a strong writer, hire an agency that includes copywriting or budget for a separate copywriter.</p>

        <h3>What happens after the website launches?</h3>
        <p>Launch is just the beginning. Your site needs security updates, performance monitoring, content updates, and continuous optimization based on real user data. Ask every agency about their post-launch support plan before you sign. An agency that disappears after launch is leaving you with a depreciating asset.</p>

        <h3>How do I know if an agency is using templates instead of custom design?</h3>
        <p>Ask directly: "Will this be a fully custom design, or will you use a pre-built theme?" Ask to see wireframes and design files before development begins. Custom designs start from scratch in Figma or a similar design tool. Template-based work starts with an existing theme that is modified. If the agency is evasive about this question, they are probably using templates.</p>

        <p class="conclusion">Choosing a web design agency is not about finding the cheapest option or the prettiest portfolio. It is about finding a strategic partner who understands your business, communicates clearly, and builds websites that generate measurable results. Use the checklist, ask the hard questions, and trust the process. The right agency is worth the effort to find.</p>

